## Proof

**Claim:** Every nonsingular $n \times n$ real matrix can be made singular by changing exactly one entry.

**Proof.**

Let $A = (a_{ij})$ be a nonsingular $n \times n$ real matrix, so $\det(A) \neq 0$.

**Key identity.** Fix a position $(i,j)$. The determinant of $A$, expanded along row $i$, is

$$\det(A) = \sum_{k=1}^{n} a_{ik}\, C_{ik},$$

where $C_{ik}$ is the $(i,k)$-cofactor of $A$. Crucially, each cofactor $C_{ik}$ is (up to sign) the determinant of the submatrix obtained by deleting row $i$ and column $k$; in particular, **none of the cofactors $C_{ik}$ depend on the entry $a_{ij}$**, since row $i$ is deleted in every minor $M_{ik}$.

Now replace the single entry $a_{ij}$ by a real number $x$, leaving all other entries unchanged, and call the resulting matrix $A'$. Then

$$\det(A') = x\, C_{ij} + \sum_{k \neq j} a_{ik}\, C_{ik} = \det(A) + (x - a_{ij})\, C_{ij}.$$

So $\det(A')$ is an affine (in fact linear in the shift) function of $x$, with slope $C_{ij}$.

**Making it singular.** We want $\det(A') = 0$, i.e.,

$$x = a_{ij} - \frac{\det(A)}{C_{ij}},$$

which is solvable in $\mathbb{R}$ provided $C_{ij} \neq 0$. Moreover, since $\det(A) \neq 0$ and $C_{ij} \neq 0$, the new value $x$ satisfies $x \neq a_{ij}$, so this is a **genuine change** of exactly one entry.

**Existence of a nonzero cofactor.** It remains to show that every nonsingular matrix has at least one nonzero cofactor. Recall the adjugate matrix $\operatorname{adj}(A)$, whose $(j,i)$-entry is $C_{ij}$. The fundamental identity is

$$A \cdot \operatorname{adj}(A) = \det(A)\, I.$$

Since $\det(A) \neq 0$, we can write

$$\operatorname{adj}(A) = \det(A)\, A^{-1}.$$

Both $\det(A) \neq 0$ and $A^{-1}$ (being the inverse of a nonsingular matrix) are nonsingular, so $\operatorname{adj}(A)$ is nonsingular. In particular, $\operatorname{adj}(A)$ is not the zero matrix, so at least one cofactor $C_{ij}$ is nonzero.

**Conclusion.** For any nonsingular $A$, select any $(i,j)$ with $C_{ij} \neq 0$ (guaranteed to exist by the argument above), and change $a_{ij}$ to $x = a_{ij} - \det(A)/C_{ij}$. This changes exactly one entry, and the resulting matrix $A'$ satisfies $\det(A') = 0$, i.e., $A'$ is singular. $\blacksquare$

